The Joint Terrorism Analysis Centre ([JTAC](https://www.mi5.gov.uk/about-us/joint-terrorism-analysis-centre)) is the United Kingdom’s independent authority for all-source terrorism assessment. JTAC is based within Military Intelligence, Section 5 ([MI5](https://www.mi5.gov.uk/about-us)), the UK's domestic counter-intelligence and security agency.&#x20;

On April 30, 2026, [the JTAC raised the UK National Threat Level](https://www.gov.uk/government/news/uk-national-threat-level-raised-to-severe) (England, Wales, Scotland and Northern Ireland) from SUBSTANTIAL, meaning an attack is likely, to SEVERE, meaning an attack is highly likely.

> The increase in threat comes following the stabbing in Golders Green in North London, but it is not solely a result of that attack. The terrorist threat level in the UK has been rising for some time, driven by an increase in the broader Islamist and Extreme Right Wing terrorist threat from individuals and small groups based in the UK.
> &#x20;
> While the UK National Threat Level set independently by JTAC reflects the terrorist threat in the UK, it comes against a backdrop of increased state-linked physical threats which is encouraging acts of violence, including against the Jewish community. This is an independent, systematic, and rigorous process, based on the very latest intelligence and analysis of internal and external factors which drive the threat.

In reaching an assessment on the appropriate threat level, a number of factors are considered including available intelligence, terrorist capability, terrorist intentions, and timescale.&#x20;

Since 2006, information about the national threat level has been [published](https://www.mi5.gov.uk/threats-and-advice/terrorism-threat-levels). In July 2019 changes were made to the terrorism threat level system, to reflect the threat posed by all forms of terrorism, irrespective of ideology.

The [United States–Mexico–Canada Agreement](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States%E2%80%93Mexico%E2%80%93Canada_Agreement) is a free trade agreement between the three countries. On June 10th, US President Trump [stated](https://www.reuters.com/world/trump-says-he-might-not-renew-usmca-2026-06-10/) that he may not renew the USMCA going into the July 1st joint review. Trump stated a similar comment [again](https://x.com/i/status/2067328399564054963) on June 17th.

If the USMCA is not extended for another 16 years during the joint review, then the agreement switches to annual joint reviews for 10 years before the agreement officially ends.

However, a USMCA member can also unilaterally leave the agreement using [Article 34.6](https://ustr.gov/sites/default/files/files/agreements/FTA/USMCA/Text/34_Final_Provisions.pdf): *A Party may withdraw from this Agreement by providing written notice of withdrawal to the other Parties. A withdrawal shall take effect six months after a Party provides written notice to the other Parties. If a Party withdraws, this Agreement shall remain in force for the remaining Parties.*&#x20;

The High Representative for Bosnia and Herzegovina, together with the Office of the High Representative (OHR) in Bosnia and Herzegovina, was created in 1995 immediately after the signing of the [Dayton Agreement](https://www.ohr.int/dayton-peace-agreement/ "Dayton Agreement") ([Annex 10](https://www.ohr.int/dayton-peace-agreement/annex-10/)), which ended the 1992-1995 [Bosnian War](https://www.britannica.com/event/Bosnian-War "Bosnian War"). The purpose of the High Representative and the OHR is to oversee the civilian implementation of the Dayton Agreement. The High Representative is appointed by and serves to represent the countries of the [Peace Implementation Council](https://www.ohr.int/international-community-in-bih/peace-implementation-council/) (PIC).

On May 11, 2026, after nearly five years as High Representative, Christian Schmidt decided to [conclude his service](https://www.ohr.int/christian-schmidt-concludes-his-mandate-as-high-representative-for-bosnia-and-herzegovina/): he "informed the PIC Steering Board of his decision and requested that they begin the work of finding his successor." In the lead-up to this decision, Schmidt had [repeatedly clashed](https://apnews.com/article/bosnia-international-envoy-resigns-serbs-a67c7c974cd1642a36831efba66929ba) with Bosnian Serb leader Milorad Dodik. Additionally, Schmidt, who had [never been approved by Russia](https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cg5pvj5pg51o) (a member of the PIC Steering Board, though it has not participated since July 2021), had apparently lost the support of the United States as well.&#x20;

Schmidt says he will [remain in office until a successor is selected](https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cg5pvj5pg51o). Meanwhile, Russia has repeatedly supported Dodik's calls for OHR to be closed altogether.

On February 28, 2026, the United States in coordination with Israel [launched](https://www.britannica.com/event/2026-Iran-war) a massive aerial campaign against Iran. The Trump administration initially [justified](https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/trump-administration/trump-administration-iran-statements-rcna263243) the attack based on national security grounds, although reporting from the [Washington Post](https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2026/02/28/trump-iran-decision-saudi-arabia-israel/) and [New York Times](https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/07/us/politics/trump-iran-war.html) have indicated that pressure from Saudi Arabia and Israel may have also influenced the decision.&#x20;

A temporary ceasefire was announced on April 8, 2026. Since then it has remained shaky with continued sporadic exchanges of violence, though was [renewed](https://apnews.com/article/us-iran-war-pakistan-april-21-2026-177a2d0701ef172c3e51686bc1f18f30) on April 21st.&#x20;

On June 3, 2026, the US House of Representatives passed, by a vote of 218 to 205, [H.Con.Res.86](https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-concurrent-resolution/86) which is now headed to the Senate. Some senators have [speculated](https://www.wtkr.com/news/in-the-community/hampton/i-think-youll-see-the-vote-next-week-after-house-passes-war-powers-resolution-vote-in-senate-could-be-coming) that a vote by the Senate on the House resolution could take place as soon as the second week of June, although the Senate version might take longer. According to Nate Silver's[ How popular is the Iran War?](https://www.natesilver.net/p/iran-war-polls-popularity-approval?utm_source=publication-search) tracker, net support for the conflict among Americans is -22.8%.&#x20;

&#x20;

On June 30, 2026, NPR [retracted](https://www.npr.org/2026/06/30/nx-s1-4622951/samuel-alito-retires) a story reporting that Samuel Alito, age 76, was retiring. There has been similar [speculation](https://www.cbsnews.com/news/supreme-court-justices-alito-thomas-not-retiring-sources-say/) about Clarence Thomas, 78, retiring. Amy Coney Barrett, though only 54, has faced [calls](https://www.dailymail.com/news/article-15942533/trump-amy-coney-barrett-birthright-citizenship.html) from conservative media to resign due to her opinions on recent cases such as birthright citizenship and whether the executive branch of the federal government can overrulse state laws on mail-in balloting.&#x20;

Supreme Court justices serve a [life tenure](https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/analysis-opinion/life-tenure-us-supreme-court-justices-global-oddity-clear-costs) and are confirmed by a simple majority of the Senate. Thus, if Democrats were to take control of the Senate in the [2026 elections,](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_elections) President Donald Trump would need Democratic senators to approve of any of his Supreme Court nominations.&#x20;

At the time of this question, Republicans control the U.S. Senate 53-47, a  majority of 6 seats. However, Metaculus [assigns](https://www.metaculus.com/questions/36370/us-senate-plurality-after-2026-midterms/) Republicans only a 55% chance of retaining the chamber. Republicans face a difficult [map](https://www.270towin.com/2026-senate-election/) in the 2026 cycle, defending contested seats in North Carolina, Georgia, Ohio, Maine, and perhaps Texas and Iowa. Democrats  are defending New Hampshire and Michigan. Historically, the out-of-power party often has an advantage in midterm elections. (See, e.g., the Brookings analysis [What history tells us about the 2026 midterm elections](https://www.brookings.edu/articles/what-history-tells-us-about-the-2026-midterm-elections/).)

According to [Reuters](https://www.reuters.com/world/india/india-us-trade-deal-be-finalised-after-ustr-section-301-probe-india-trade-2026-06-08/), the United States and India are finalising talks on an interim trade deal with Washington.

> The two countries reached an [initial understanding](https://www.reuters.com/world/india/us-india-release-framework-an-interim-trade-deal-2026-02-06/) on a trade deal in February 2026, but negotiations slowed after U.S. President Donald Trump's sweeping tariff measures were struck ​down by the U.S. Supreme Court.
> &#x20;
> The US has since proposed an ​additional tariff of 12.5% on imports from India, and other countries, ⁠due to what Washington says is these countries' use of forced ​labour. It is also looking at imposing a separate tariff on India, claiming ​India has excess capacity in industries such as textiles and is exporting too much to the United States, damaging US industry.
> &#x20;
> &#x20;
> Bilateral trade [talks](https://www.reuters.com/world/india/india-us-near-trade-pact-tariff-terms-hinge-section-301-relief-source-says-2026-06-01/) gathered pace last week ​as a U.S. delegation led by Assistant US Trade ​Representative for ⁠South and Central Asia Brendan Lynch held three days of talks with Indian trade officials in New Delhi.
> &#x20;
> Last week, India's trade minister [Piyush Goyal said](https://www.reuters.com/world/india/first-tranche-us-india-trade-deal-likely-by-mid-july-says-india-trade-minister-2026-06-05/) the two ⁠sides ​were moving swiftly toward finalising the first ​tranche of a bilateral trade agreement that could be concluded by mid-July.

Space tourism company Virgin Galactic, founded by Richard Branson in 2004, has seen its stock price plunge 99% from its 2019 IPO, as the company struggles under the burden of debt and burning cash.

According to the company in its [Q1 2026 10-Q](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1706946/000170694626000068/spce-20260331.htm), it is a race against the clock:&#x20;

> We are continuing the production of our next-generation spaceships and are progressing through our build milestones. We currently expect our flight test program to commence in the third quarter of 2026 in advance of restarting commercial service, which is expected to begin in the fourth quarter of 2026 with a research flight. We currently expect private astronaut spaceflights to commence six to eight weeks after our first commercial spaceflight.

In that same filing, Virgin Galactic had the follow Going Concern statement:&#x20;

> Management’s evaluation, which excluded the items that are not within its control (i.e., Revenues, Capital Market Transactions, and Debt Repayment Terms), resulted in the determination that the Company may not have sufficient cash and marketable securities to maintain its planned operations for the next twelve months following the issuance date of the condensed consolidated financial statements and has concluded that there are conditions present in the aggregate that raise substantial doubt about the Company’s ability to continue as a going concern pursuant to ASC 205-40.
